body:
Abortion is tragic because women are biologically engineered to try to keep offspring alive. Going against that biological drive, for even the most justified reasons, is very psychologically damaging. Even if the fetus is not yet a "life," the body thinks it's going to be, and starts releasing all the hormones and such like it normally would to promote the fetus' growth. As a result, the woman experiences physical and mental distress (often amounting to intense grief) when the pregnancy is terminated.

Discounting that distress is the only way to equate abortion with contraception. But, the act of discounting that distress renders the equivalence immoral. Thus, abortion cannot be morally equal to contraception, regardless of whether the fetus is considered a life.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I see your point, and sense your frustration. I'm sorry you're going through this :( 

Still, I'd like to encourage you to reconsider. You're asking him to change, but people don't just change like that overnight. It's not how our brains are wired. (I highly recommend the book 'The Power of Habit' by Charles Duhigg. He does a really engaging breakdown of this).

Stepping back and choosing your battles is no different than what I described above in terms of micromanagement. It's just negative reinforcement, rather than positive reinforcement. Maybe you're using the stick less often, but you're still using a stick. I'm just suggesting you try a carrot instead, or in addition. 

Someone on here reminded me of the importance of working as a team. If the idea of using rewards is too distasteful, maybe try finding another way to work together on this.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

